    <div class="section-label">The short answer</div>
    <h2 id="overview-h">What group health insurance costs per employee</h2>
    <div class="answer"><p class="answer-p">Across 3,500+ companies insured through Plum, half spend between roughly ₹6,000 and ₹24,000 per employee per year (PEPY) on group health insurance. A quarter spend less than ₹6,000, and a quarter spend more than ₹24,000, mostly because they cover parents or carry a higher sum insured. Where a specific company lands depends on sum insured, family definition, employee age mix, industry risk, claims history at renewal, and the add-ons chosen.</p>
<p class="answer-p">The ranges on this page are directional. A specific quote for your company will sit somewhere inside them once an insurer sees your employee data, and that number can move 20 to 40 percent either way based on the design choices discussed below.</p></div>
  </section>

<div class="rule-section" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div><div class="chapter-head" id="chapter-1"><span class="ch-num" aria-hidden="true">01</span><div><div class="section-label">Chapter 1 of 6</div><p class="ch-title">What companies spend</p><p class="ch-desc">Real ranges from Plum customer data, by company type, and what the top quarter buys.</p><ul class="ch-links"><li><a href="#what-companies-spend">What companies spend</a></li><li><a href="#how-your-peers-compare">Startups, MNCs and size</a></li><li><a href="#what-the-top-quarter-does-differently">What top spenders cover</a></li></ul></div></div><section id="what-companies-spend" class="sec" aria-labelledby="what-companies-spend-h"><div class="section-label">Part 2 · What companies spend</div><h2 id="what-companies-spend-h">What do Indian companies actually spend on group health insurance?</h2><p class="section-intro"><strong>Half of companies spend between roughly ₹6,000 and ₹24,000 per employee per year.</strong> The picture shifts by company type — MNCs and funded startups spend more, small businesses track the overall spread. The chart below compares a typical company of each type.</p>

<figure class="spend-chart" id="how-your-peers-compare">
  <figcaption>What a typical company spends on group health insurance, per employee a year</figcaption>
  <ol class="sc-rows"><li class="sc-row"><span class="sc-name">MNCs</span><span class="sc-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sc-bar" style="width:86.7%"></span></span><span class="sc-val"><strong>₹26,000</strong> typical<span class="sc-range">most spend ₹12k–₹50k</span></span></li><li class="sc-row"><span class="sc-name">Funded startups</span><span class="sc-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sc-bar" style="width:56.7%"></span></span><span class="sc-val"><strong>₹17,000</strong> typical<span class="sc-range">most spend ₹7k–₹35k</span></span></li><li class="sc-row"><span class="sc-name">Large companies (200+)</span><span class="sc-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sc-bar" style="width:46.7%"></span></span><span class="sc-val"><strong>₹14,000</strong> typical<span class="sc-range">most spend ₹6k–₹30k</span></span></li><li class="sc-row"><span class="sc-name">Growing companies (51–200)</span><span class="sc-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sc-bar" style="width:40.0%"></span></span><span class="sc-val"><strong>₹12,000</strong> typical<span class="sc-range">most spend ₹6k–₹28k</span></span></li><li class="sc-row sc-all"><span class="sc-name">All companies</span><span class="sc-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sc-bar" style="width:33.3%"></span></span><span class="sc-val"><strong>₹10,000</strong> typical<span class="sc-range">most spend ₹6k–₹24k</span></span></li><li class="sc-row"><span class="sc-name">Small businesses (7–50)</span><span class="sc-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sc-bar" style="width:30.0%"></span></span><span class="sc-val"><strong>₹9,000</strong> typical<span class="sc-range">most spend ₹6k–₹22k</span></span></li></ol>
  <p class="sc-note">"Typical" is the median company. "Most spend" is the middle half of companies, from the 25th to the 75th percentile. Plum customer data, FY26.</p>
  <p class="sc-insight">MNCs spend the most: a typical MNC spends ₹26,000 per employee, more than twice the ₹10,000 typical across all companies.</p>
</figure>

<p class="start"><strong>Where to start.</strong> Divide your current GMC premium by your headcount and compare it with the typical figure and range for your company type above.</p>

<div class="detail-body deep-acc-body"><div class="callout callout-blue"><span aria-hidden="true" class="callout-icon">ℹ</span><div><strong>About this data:</strong> Figures come from the group health policies of 3,500+ companies insured through Plum in FY26. They show the base group medical cover (GMC) premium per employee per year, before life, accident or wellness benefits, grouped into five indicative spend ranges.</div></div><p>The most common range across all companies is ₹5,000–10,000, where about 3 in 10 companies sit. About 1 in 8 spend over ₹40,000, and more than 9 in 10 of those cover employees' parents.</p><p>Use these ranges as a starting check, not a target. Sitting in the lower quarter is not a problem in itself, and a lean policy can be the right call for a young team. The chart above shows how the picture shifts by company type.</p></div>
</section>


<div class="rule-section lav" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div>
<section id="what-the-top-quarter-does-differently" class="sec" aria-labelledby="what-the-top-quarter-does-differently-h"><div class="section-label">Part 4 · Policy design</div><h2 id="what-the-top-quarter-does-differently-h">What do the highest-spending companies cover?</h2><p class="section-intro"><strong>The top quarter of companies spend over ₹24,000 per employee, and most of that comes down to policy design.</strong> More than 9 in 10 top-quarter companies cover parents, carry a sum insured of ₹7.5 lakh or more, or both.</p><figure class="cmp-card">
<figcaption>Share of companies with each feature</figcaption>
<div class="cmp-legend"><span class="li"><span class="sw top"></span>Top quarter (over ₹24,000)</span><span class="li"><span class="sw rest"></span>Everyone else</span></div>
<div class="cmp-row"><div class="cmp-label">Cover parents</div>
<div class="cmp-bars"><div class="cmp-bar top" style="width:90%"><span>90%</span></div>
<div class="cmp-bar rest" style="width:11%"><span>11%</span></div></div></div><div class="cmp-row"><div class="cmp-label">Sum insured of ₹7.5 lakh or more</div>
<div class="cmp-bars"><div class="cmp-bar top" style="width:33%"><span>33%</span></div>
<div class="cmp-bar rest" style="width:7%"><span>7%</span></div></div></div><div class="cmp-row"><div class="cmp-label">Include maternity</div>
<div class="cmp-bars"><div class="cmp-bar top" style="width:86%"><span>86%</span></div>
<div class="cmp-bar rest" style="width:55%"><span>55%</span></div></div></div>
</figure><p class="start"><strong>Where to start.</strong> Decide which of these three your employees would use most, then price that one first. <a href="https://www.plumhq.com/get-quote">See where your current policy sits against companies like yours</a>.</p><div class="detail-body deep-acc-body"><p>None of these is right for every company. Parental cover adds the most cost, so it is worth knowing how many of your employees would use it. A higher sum insured matters most where a single hospitalisation could exhaust ₹5 lakh. Maternity matters most for a workforce at family-building age. The useful question is which of these your employees would value enough to justify the cost.</p></div></section><div class="rule-section" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div><div class="chapter-head" id="chapter-2"><span class="ch-num" aria-hidden="true">02</span><div><div class="section-label">Chapter 2 of 6</div><p class="ch-title">How pricing works</p><p class="ch-desc">The inputs insurers price on, and why the same company can land anywhere in the range.</p><ul class="ch-links"><li><a href="#how-group-health-insurance-is-priced-in-india">How GHI is priced</a></li><li><a href="#cost-benchmarks-by-sum-insured">Cost by sum insured</a></li><li><a href="#why-the-same-company-lands-at-different-points-in-the-range">Why quotes vary</a></li><li><a href="#whats-included-in-a-base-policy-and-what-add-ons-cost">Base policy and add-ons</a></li><li><a href="#industry-profile-and-how-it-moves-cost">Industry profile</a></li></ul></div></div><section id="how-group-health-insurance-is-priced-in-india" class="sec" aria-labelledby="how-group-health-insurance-is-priced-in-india-h"><div class="section-label">Part 5 · How pricing works</div><h2 id="how-group-health-insurance-is-priced-in-india-h">How group health insurance is priced in India</h2><p class="section-intro">Insurers price a group health policy using eight main inputs. Any one of these can move the premium by 10 to 50 percent, and the interaction between them is what produces the width of the ranges in this guide.</p><div class="detail-body deep-acc-body"><h3>Sum insured</h3><p>The headline cover amount per employee or family floater. Common tiers in the Indian market are ₹1 lakh, ₹2 lakh, ₹3 lakh, ₹5 lakh, ₹7.5 lakh, and ₹10 lakh and above. Doubling the sum insured does not double the premium; the relationship is closer to a 40 to 60 percent uplift at each step because claims severity concentrates at the lower end of the hospitalisation cost distribution.</p><h3>Family definition</h3><p>The combination of dependents covered. The main configurations are:</p><ul>
<li><strong>E</strong>: employee only</li>
<li><strong>E+S</strong>: employee and spouse</li>
<li><strong>E+S+C</strong>: employee, spouse, and children</li>
<li><strong>E+S+C+P</strong>: employee, spouse, children, and parents (or parents-in-law)</li>
</ul><p>Moving from E to E+S+C typically adds 60 to 110 percent to premium. Adding parents can add another 40 to 120 percent on top of that, depending on parent age bands and whether the cover is floater or individual.</p><h3>Age and gender profile of the covered population</h3><p>A workforce with a median age of 28 will price materially lower than one with a median age of 40, and a workforce with higher female representation will price higher for policies that include maternity. Insurers model age-bracket distributions, not averages, so a bimodal workforce (many under-30s and a leadership layer in their 50s) prices differently from a flat profile at the same mean age.</p><h3>Industry and occupational risk</h3><p>Office-based roles sit at the low end of the risk curve. Manufacturing, logistics, field sales, and construction sit higher. Insurers apply loading of 5 to 25 percent for industries with elevated accident or occupational-illness profiles. Tech, BFSI, and professional services companies therefore see a different pricing floor than light manufacturing of the same size.</p><h3>Claims history (from renewal year 2 onward)</h3><p>For the first-year policy, insurers price on employee demographics and policy design. From the second renewal, actual claims experience takes over as the dominant input. A company with a loss ratio above 85 percent in year 1 should expect a 20 to 45 percent renewal hike in year 2, independent of market movement. A loss ratio under 50 percent can hold renewal increases to 5 to 15 percent.</p><h3>Add-ons and riders</h3><p>Maternity benefit, parental cover, OPD, mental health, modern treatments, infertility cover, critical illness riders, and personal accident each have their own cost structure. The impact of each as a percentage of base premium is covered in a dedicated section below.</p><h3>Geography and hospital network</h3><p>Metro-heavy workforces price higher than tier-2 or tier-3 heavy workforces because urban hospital tariffs are higher. The cashless hospital network available to a given policy depends on the insurer; Plum works with multiple insurers including ICICI Lombard, HDFC ERGO, Bajaj Allianz, Star Health, Niva Bupa, and Aditya Birla Health Insurance, each with distinct network footprints.</p><h3>Group size</h3><p>Larger groups get better rates because of risk pooling and broker leverage with insurers. Plum writes GHI policies starting at a minimum group size of 7 employees, which is lower than most market defaults. The pricing effect of group size is explored in the next section.</p></div></section>
<div class="rule-section lav" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div>
<section id="cost-benchmarks-by-sum-insured" class="sec" aria-labelledby="cost-benchmarks-by-sum-insured-h"><div class="section-label">Part 6 · Sum insured</div><h2 id="cost-benchmarks-by-sum-insured-h">Cost benchmarks by sum insured</h2><p class="section-intro">The table below shows indicative PEPY ranges for the same E+S+C family definition at different sum insured tiers, for a mid-size company (51 to 500 employees) in a low-risk industry.</p><div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Sum insured per family</th>
<th>Indicative PEPY range</th>
<th>Typical use case</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>₹1 lakh</td>
<td>₹2,500 to ₹5,000</td>
<td>Statutory minimum top-up, blue-collar workforce</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>₹2 lakh</td>
<td>₹3,500 to ₹7,000</td>
<td>Entry-level cover, often paired with ESIC</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>₹3 lakh</td>
<td>₹4,500 to ₹8,500</td>
<td>Common SMB and startup baseline</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>₹5 lakh</td>
<td>₹6,500 to ₹12,000</td>
<td>Standard mid-market cover</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>₹7.5 lakh</td>
<td>₹9,000 to ₹16,000</td>
<td>Richer mid-market and tech companies</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>₹10 lakh</td>
<td>₹12,000 to ₹20,000</td>
<td>Senior-weighted workforces, leadership tiers</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>₹10 lakh+</td>
<td>₹15,000 to ₹30,000+</td>
<td>Leadership-only or super top-up structures</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div><div class="detail-body deep-acc-body"><p>The per-rupee cost of cover drops as sum insured rises. A ₹10 lakh policy does not cost five times a ₹2 lakh policy because the probability of a claim exceeding ₹2 lakh is much lower than the probability of any claim at all.</p></div></section>
<div class="rule-section lav" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div>
<section id="why-the-same-company-lands-at-different-points-in-the-range" class="sec" aria-labelledby="why-the-same-company-lands-at-different-points-in-the-range-h"><div class="section-label">Part 7 · What moves your quote</div><h2 id="why-the-same-company-lands-at-different-points-in-the-range-h">Why the same company lands at different points in the range</h2><p class="section-intro">A single 150-person tech company in Bengaluru can get quoted anywhere from ₹5,500 to ₹13,000 PEPY for what sounds like the same policy. The illustration below shows the levers that produce that spread for the same company.</p><div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Scenario</th>
<th>PEPY</th>
<th>What's different</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Low end</td>
<td>~₹5,500</td>
<td>₹3 lakh SI, E only, no maternity, 25 percent co-pay, ₹5,000 room-rent cap, 2-year disease waiting period kept</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Mid</td>
<td>~₹8,500</td>
<td>₹5 lakh SI, E+S+C, maternity included, no co-pay, single private room, waiting periods waived</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>High end</td>
<td>~₹13,000</td>
<td>₹5 lakh SI, E+S+C, maternity + parental (one parent) + OPD, no co-pay, no sub-limits, modern treatments rider</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div><div class="detail-body deep-acc-body"><p>The low-end policy is cheaper on the invoice. It also pushes substantial cost onto employees at the moment of claim, which is covered in a later section.</p></div></section>
<div class="rule-section lav" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div>
<section id="whats-included-in-a-base-policy-and-what-add-ons-cost" class="sec" aria-labelledby="whats-included-in-a-base-policy-and-what-add-ons-cost-h"><div class="section-label">Part 8 · Coverage and add-ons</div><h2 id="whats-included-in-a-base-policy-and-what-add-ons-cost-h">What's included in a base policy and what add-ons cost</h2><p class="section-intro">A base GHI policy typically includes in-patient hospitalisation (minimum 24 hours), day-care procedures, pre-hospitalisation expenses (30 to 60 days), post-hospitalisation expenses (60 to 90 days), ambulance charges, and domiciliary hospitalisation under defined conditions. ICU, room rent, and surgeon fees are included within sub-limits that the policy design specifies.</p><div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Add-on</th>
<th>Uplift on base premium</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Maternity benefit</td>
<td>+10% to +25%</td>
<td>Varies with waiting period, cap amount, and infant cover duration</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Parental cover (floater, both parents)</td>
<td>+40% to +120%</td>
<td>Highest-impact add-on; see dedicated section below</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>OPD cover</td>
<td>+8% to +18%</td>
<td>Wallet model usually cheaper than claim-reimbursement model</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Mental health cover</td>
<td>+2% to +6%</td>
<td>Mandatory IRDAI inclusion for in-patient; OPD mental health is add-on</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Modern treatments rider</td>
<td>+1% to +3%</td>
<td>Robotic surgery, oral chemotherapy, stem-cell therapy, 10 to 12 named procedures</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Infertility cover</td>
<td>+3% to +8%</td>
<td>IVF and ART; typically capped and age-restricted</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Personal accident cover</td>
<td>+2% to +5%</td>
<td>Separate benefit, not a loading on base</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Critical illness rider</td>
<td>+4% to +10%</td>
<td>Lump-sum benefit on diagnosis of listed conditions</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div><div class="detail-body deep-acc-body"><p>Add-ons sit on top. The table above shows the typical uplift as a percentage of base premium for each add-on.</p><p>Companies evaluating add-ons should map each one to actual employee utilisation data from the previous year, if available, rather than selecting from the menu by intuition.</p></div></section>
<div class="rule-section lav" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div>
<section id="industry-profile-and-how-it-moves-cost" class="sec" aria-labelledby="industry-profile-and-how-it-moves-cost-h"><div class="section-label">Part 9 · Industry</div><h2 id="industry-profile-and-how-it-moves-cost-h">Industry profile and how it moves cost</h2><div class="detail-body deep-acc-body"><ul>
<li><strong>Technology and SaaS</strong>: sits at the baseline. Low-risk occupation, young workforce, metro-concentrated. The PEPY ranges in the tables above apply directly.</li>
<li><strong>BFSI</strong>: similar to tech on occupational risk, but older median age in traditional banking segments shifts PEPY 10 to 20 percent higher.</li>
<li><strong>Professional services (consulting, legal, accounting)</strong>: tech-adjacent for occupational risk, with higher travel and stress-related claim patterns that show up at year-2 renewal.</li>
<li><strong>Manufacturing and industrial</strong>: 15 to 25 percent loading on PEPY compared to tech, driven by accident risk and higher incidence of back and joint claims.</li>
<li><strong>Logistics and field sales</strong>: 10 to 20 percent loading, with policy design often needing personal accident cover as a mandatory layer.</li>
<li><strong>Healthcare and pharma</strong>: variable; office-based pharma prices as tech, hospital staff prices as manufacturing.</li>
<li><strong>Retail and hospitality</strong>: moderate loading, with higher attrition driving administrative load that affects total cost of ownership.</li>
</ul></div></section><div class="rule-section" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div><div class="chapter-head" id="chapter-3"><span class="ch-num" aria-hidden="true">03</span><div><div class="section-label">Chapter 3 of 6</div><p class="ch-title">The full cost</p><p class="ch-desc">What the premium leaves out: renewals, risk transfer, peer benchmarks and admin load.</p><ul class="ch-links"><li><a href="#beyond-the-sticker-price-three-cost-realities-most-buyers-mi">Beyond the sticker price: three cost realities most buyers miss</a></li><li><a href="#the-cfo-view-group-health-insurance-as-risk-transfer">The CFO view</a></li><li><a href="#benchmarking-against-the-right-peer-set">Peer-set benchmarking</a></li><li><a href="#admin-load-and-total-cost-of-ownership">Admin load and TCO</a></li></ul></div></div><section id="beyond-the-sticker-price-three-cost-realities-most-buyers-mi" class="sec" aria-labelledby="beyond-the-sticker-price-three-cost-realities-most-buyers-mi-h"><div class="section-label">Part 10 · Hidden costs</div><h2 id="beyond-the-sticker-price-three-cost-realities-most-buyers-mi-h">Beyond the sticker price: three cost realities most buyers miss</h2><p class="section-intro">The premium printed on an insurer quote is one number. The actual cost of operating a group health insurance programme over three years is a different number. Three realities drive the gap.</p><div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Design choice to cut premium</th>
<th>Premium saving</th>
<th>What the employee pays at claim time</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Add 20% co-pay on all claims</td>
<td>10% to 15%</td>
<td>20% of every hospitalisation bill, cash at discharge</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Add ₹5,000 room-rent cap</td>
<td>5% to 10%</td>
<td>Proportional deduction on all associated bills if they pick a higher-category room</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Keep 2-year disease-waiting period</td>
<td>3% to 7%</td>
<td>Full out-of-pocket cost for listed conditions in first 2 years</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Add 10% disease-wise sub-limits (cataract, hernia, etc.)</td>
<td>2% to 5%</td>
<td>Any cost above the sub-limit cap</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Exclude maternity</td>
<td>10% to 25%</td>
<td>Full delivery cost (₹60,000 to ₹3 lakh) or shift to spouse's policy</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div><div class="detail-body deep-acc-body"><h3>Year 3 is priced by your year 1 design choices</h3><p>Claims experience is the dominant input to year 2 and year 3 pricing, and claims experience is shaped by year 1 design choices. A company that buys the cheapest possible first-year policy, with high co-pays and tight sub-limits, often sees worse claims outcomes (because employees delay care, or because claims hit cost caps and get partially denied) and still ends up with high renewal hikes because the loss ratio reads badly on paper.</p><p>A buyer picking between a ₹5,500 PEPY policy and a ₹7,500 PEPY policy for the same company should model the three-year total, not just year 1. The cheaper policy frequently ends up more expensive by year 3.</p><h3>Cost drifts after the policy starts</h3><p>The PEPY you sign for is the opening balance. Mid-year endorsements add 10 to 20 percent to the annual outlay for most growing companies.</p><ul>
<li><strong>New joiner additions</strong>: pro-rated against the annual premium, often at a slightly higher per-month rate than the base.</li>
<li><strong>Mid-term dependent additions</strong> (marriages, births, newly covered parents): typically full annual premium for the dependent, not pro-rated.</li>
<li><strong>Mid-year sum insured upgrades</strong>: full differential charged, with underwriting at the new tier.</li>
<li><strong>Add-on mid-term activation</strong> (new OPD wallet, extended maternity): full differential plus any administration fee the broker or insurer charges.</li>
</ul><p>A finance team that budgets at the quoted PEPY and does not reserve 10 to 20 percent for in-year drift typically ends the policy year over budget.</p><h3>When you cut premium, you transfer cost to employees</h3><p>Every rupee shaved off the premium gets paid by someone. For design choices that cut premium, the person paying is the employee at the moment of claim.</p><p>A premium cut via co-pay and sub-limits is not a cost reduction. It is a transfer from the employer's P&amp;L to the employee's bank account, triggered at the worst possible moment for the employee.</p></div></section>
<div class="rule-section lav" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div>
<section id="the-cfo-view-group-health-insurance-as-risk-transfer" class="sec" aria-labelledby="the-cfo-view-group-health-insurance-as-risk-transfer-h"><div class="section-label">Part 11 · For finance teams</div><h2 id="the-cfo-view-group-health-insurance-as-risk-transfer-h">The CFO view: group health insurance as risk transfer</h2><p class="section-intro">A CFO framing of GHI treats the premium as the price of transferring tail risk. The question is not "what does GHI cost per employee" but "what is the probability-weighted cost of absorbing catastrophic health events on the balance sheet versus paying a known annual premium to transfer that risk."</p><div class="detail-body deep-acc-body"><p>For a 100-person company:</p><ul>
<li>Expected hospitalisation incidence per year: 6 to 12 events</li>
<li>Expected high-severity events (above ₹5 lakh): 0 to 2 per year</li>
<li>Expected catastrophic events (above ₹15 lakh): a non-zero probability every 2 to 4 years</li>
<li>Without GHI: these costs land on the employer (if absorbed voluntarily) or on employees (if not), with the second case producing substantial attrition and productivity cost</li>
</ul><p>The GHI premium at 100 people (roughly ₹5 lakh to ₹12 lakh for a baseline policy) is small relative to the variance it absorbs. For a CFO, the premium range is less interesting than the smoothness of the P&amp;L outcome and the insulation from a bad-luck quarter where one leader needs cardiac surgery and two employees have complicated deliveries.</p></div></section>
<div class="rule-section lav" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div>
<section id="benchmarking-against-the-right-peer-set" class="sec" aria-labelledby="benchmarking-against-the-right-peer-set-h"><div class="section-label">Part 12 · Benchmarking</div><h2 id="benchmarking-against-the-right-peer-set-h">Benchmarking against the right peer set</h2><p class="section-intro">Market averages are the wrong benchmark for most companies. The right benchmark is a peer set of five to ten companies with similar stage, city, hiring pool, and employee value proposition — the companies this one actually competes with for talent.</p><div class="detail-body deep-acc-body"><p>Peer-set benchmarking is tighter than market-average benchmarking. A 200-person Series B SaaS company in Bengaluru competing with other Series B SaaS companies for engineering talent should expect its GHI to sit within a ₹2,000 to ₹3,000 PEPY band of its peers, even though the middle half of all 51 to 200 employee companies on Plum spends anywhere from ₹6,000 to ₹28,000.</p><p>A buyer whose policy sits in the market range but 25 percent below their peer set has a retention risk that will not show up in a benchmarking report. One whose policy sits above their peer set has a budget conversation to have with their CFO.</p><p>The right questions to answer when building a peer set:</p><ul>
<li>Which 10 companies do we lose candidates to or win candidates from?</li>
<li>What is the median tenure of the engineering or sales team at those companies?</li>
<li>What is the published or signalled GHI structure at those companies (sum insured, family definition, parental cover presence)?</li>
</ul></div></section>
<div class="rule-section lav" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div>
<section id="admin-load-and-total-cost-of-ownership" class="sec" aria-labelledby="admin-load-and-total-cost-of-ownership-h"><div class="section-label">Part 13 · Total cost of ownership</div><h2 id="admin-load-and-total-cost-of-ownership-h">Admin load and total cost of ownership</h2><p class="section-intro">Premium is one line of the GHI cost. HR hours spent operating the programme are another. For a 100-person company, a well-run programme consumes 3 to 6 HR hours per month on steady state and 15 to 25 hours per month during renewal and open enrolment. A poorly-run programme can consume 15 to 20 hours per month on steady state alone, driven by:</p><div class="detail-body deep-acc-body"><ul>
<li>Employee queries about coverage (what's included, how to claim, which hospitals)</li>
<li>Endorsement management (new joiners, dependent additions, exits)</li>
<li>Claim escalations and insurer follow-up</li>
<li>Pre-authorisation chasing during active hospitalisations</li>
</ul><p>At an average loaded HR cost of ₹1,200 to ₹2,500 per hour, the gap between a well-run and poorly-run programme is ₹20,000 to ₹60,000 per month, or ₹2.4 lakh to ₹7.2 lakh per year on admin load alone. That number should factor into any TCO comparison across brokers.</p><p>Service quality shows up in two metrics in particular: pre-authorisation turnaround time and claim settlement experience. Plum's median pre-authorisation TAT is 45 minutes, and Plum's claims NPS is 79, both of which are above typical industry benchmarks and both of which translate directly into fewer HR escalations.</p></div></section><div class="rule-section" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div><div class="chapter-head" id="chapter-4"><span class="ch-num" aria-hidden="true">04</span><div><div class="section-label">Chapter 4 of 6</div><p class="ch-title">Levers you control</p><p class="ch-desc">Parental cover, ESIC, tiering and how to set a budget.</p><ul class="ch-links"><li><a href="#parental-cover-the-fastest-growing-and-most-under-provisione">Parental cover: the fastest-growing and most under-provisioned line</a></li><li><a href="#esic-and-group-health-insurance-together">ESIC and GHI</a></li><li><a href="#tiering-as-a-cost-lever">Tiering</a></li><li><a href="#how-to-budget-for-group-health-insurance">How to budget</a></li></ul></div></div><section id="parental-cover-the-fastest-growing-and-most-under-provisione" class="sec" aria-labelledby="parental-cover-the-fastest-growing-and-most-under-provisione-h"><div class="section-label">Part 14 · Parental cover</div><h2 id="parental-cover-the-fastest-growing-and-most-under-provisione-h">Parental cover: the fastest-growing and most under-provisioned line</h2><p class="section-intro">Parental cover is the single most impactful add-on on premium and the one that most scaling companies get wrong. Three structural facts drive the economics:</p><div class="detail-body deep-acc-body"><h3>Claim severity is 3 to 5 times higher for parents</h3><p>Parent-age populations (55 to 75) generate claims that are both more frequent and larger than employee or dependent-child claims. A parent cohort can account for 50 to 70 percent of a policy's total claim payout while representing 20 to 30 percent of covered lives.</p><h3>Renewal hikes concentrate here</h3><p>A policy with uncapped parental cover frequently sees year-2 renewal increases of 35 to 60 percent, almost entirely driven by the parent segment. Policies without parental cover see much more stable renewal pricing.</p><h3>Structuring options control cost without cutting the benefit</h3><p>Companies that want to offer parental cover without absorbing full cost have several design paths:</p><ul>
<li><strong>Voluntary / employee-paid</strong>: employer makes the policy available, employee pays the parental cover premium</li>
<li><strong>Buy-up structure</strong>: employer funds a base amount, employee tops up for parental inclusion</li>
<li><strong>Tiered sum insured</strong>: lower sum insured for parents than for employee and immediate family</li>
<li><strong>Age-banded premium sharing</strong>: employer funds a higher share for parents under 60, lower share above</li>
<li><strong>Capped out-patient and modern-treatment exclusions on the parent segment</strong></li>
</ul><p>The right structure depends on workforce demographics, employee preferences, and the signal the company wants to send about care for extended family. There is no universally correct answer.</p></div></section>
<div class="rule-section lav" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div>
<section id="esic-and-group-health-insurance-together" class="sec" aria-labelledby="esic-and-group-health-insurance-together-h"><div class="section-label">Part 15 · ESIC</div><h2 id="esic-and-group-health-insurance-together-h">ESIC and group health insurance together</h2><p class="section-intro">Employees with monthly wages up to ₹21,000 are mandatorily covered under the Employees' State Insurance Corporation (ESIC) scheme. Companies with mixed wage bands often need to run ESIC for the covered segment and private GHI for the rest, which raises three questions:</p><div class="detail-body deep-acc-body"><h3>Do I need private GHI on top of ESIC for ESIC-covered employees?</h3><p>Not legally required. Many companies choose to provide a top-up private GHI for ESIC-covered employees because ESIC hospital access is uneven across cities and procedure types, and private cover produces materially better employee experience during claims.</p><h3>How much does private cover for the ESIC-covered segment cost?</h3><p>Typically ₹1,500 to ₹4,000 PEPY for a ₹1 lakh to ₹2 lakh sum insured top-up. Lower sum insured policies have proportionally higher per-rupee premiums because of fixed administrative costs.</p><h3>Should I combine both populations into one GHI policy?</h3><p>Operationally simpler, financially more expensive. A unified policy at ₹3 lakh sum insured for everyone can cost 20 to 40 percent more than a split structure. Larger companies with HR capacity usually run split structures; smaller companies often prioritise simplicity.</p></div></section>
<div class="rule-section lav" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div>
<section id="tiering-as-a-cost-lever" class="sec" aria-labelledby="tiering-as-a-cost-lever-h"><div class="section-label">Part 16 · Cost levers</div><h2 id="tiering-as-a-cost-lever-h">Tiering as a cost lever</h2><p class="section-intro">Treating "the GHI policy" as monolithic is a common and expensive mistake. Companies that tier their GHI structure can hold total employer cost flat while materially improving perceived benefit. Three common tiering patterns:</p><div class="detail-body deep-acc-body"><h3>Role-based tiers</h3><p>Different sum insured and family definition for leadership (VP+ or founders), management (senior IC and manager), and individual contributors. Leadership tier typically has ₹10 lakh to ₹25 lakh sum insured with full parental cover; IC tier might have ₹3 lakh to ₹5 lakh sum insured with E+S+C only. Total employer cost can be 10 to 20 percent lower than offering the leadership policy to everyone.</p><h3>Voluntary top-ups</h3><p>Base policy covers everyone at a sensible floor (say ₹3 lakh SI, E+S+C), with employee-paid voluntary top-ups available for higher sum insured, parental inclusion, or OPD. The employer absorbs the base cost; the employee funds personalisation. Participation rates in voluntary top-ups typically run 30 to 55 percent among eligible employees.</p><h3>Dependent buy-ups</h3><p>Base policy covers employee only or E+S, with employees able to buy additional dependents at a defined contribution split. Common in cost-conscious startups transitioning from E-only to E+S+C.</p><p>The design choice signals values. A company that funds parental cover for everyone signals extended-family care. One that offers it as a voluntary buy-up signals personal choice. Neither is correct in the abstract; both are correct for the company whose values they reflect.</p></div></section>
<div class="rule-section lav" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div>
<section id="how-to-budget-for-group-health-insurance" class="sec" aria-labelledby="how-to-budget-for-group-health-insurance-h"><div class="section-label">Part 17 · Budgeting</div><h2 id="how-to-budget-for-group-health-insurance-h">How to budget for group health insurance</h2><p class="section-intro">Three budgeting approaches, in increasing order of sophistication:</p><ol class="steps"><li class="step"><h3>Rule of thumb: percentage of CTC</h3><p>Most Indian companies spend 0.8 to 2.5 percent of total CTC on GHI premium. Tech and professional services companies cluster at 1.2 to 1.8 percent. This method is quick but imprecise, because CTC varies with city and seniority mix in ways that do not perfectly track hospitalisation risk.</p></li><li class="step"><h3>Peer-set benchmarking</h3><p>Build a peer set of five to ten similar companies and target the median of that peer set, with explicit decisions about where to sit above or below.</p></li><li class="step"><h3>Design-first budgeting</h3><p>Start from the benefit the company wants to offer (sum insured, family definition, add-ons) and work backward to the premium. This is the right method for companies that treat GHI as a strategic lever rather than an expense line, and it is the one that typically produces the best employee outcomes.</p></li></ol><div class="detail-body deep-acc-body"><div class="callout callout-coral"><span aria-hidden="true" class="callout-icon">⚑</span><div><strong>Budget note:</strong> A full budget should include three line items: premium, administration (HR hours), and a 10 to 20 percent reserve for mid-year drift.</div></div></div></section><div class="rule-section" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div><div class="chapter-head" id="chapter-5"><span class="ch-num" aria-hidden="true">05</span><div><div class="section-label">Chapter 5 of 6</div><p class="ch-title">Benchmark your plan</p><p class="ch-desc">Pick the cover your plan includes and see where it ranks against 3,500+ companies.</p><ul class="ch-links"><li><a href="#benefits-calculator">Benefits calculator</a></li></ul></div></div><section id="benefits-calculator" class="sec" aria-labelledby="benefits-calculator-h"><div class="section-label">Part 1 · Benefits calculator</div><h2 id="benefits-calculator-h">Where does your group health plan rank?</h2><p class="section-intro"><strong>Pick the cover your plan includes and see which percentile it sits in against 3,500+ companies on Plum.</strong> The ranking uses Plum's employee benefits matrix, which scores how deep your insurance is and how broad your healthcare benefits are.</p><div class="calc" id="calc">
